#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093002} :activity/id #uuid "4afd7cce-79fd-54bd-b3c9-a093406d5b61" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049230 :topic/id #uuid "e549bd5c-fab0-54f5-872f-4565e6f0c4f2" :topic/math-academy-id 927} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093494 :tutorial/content "Recall that the **principle of induction** states the following: \n\n*Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), \\, P(2), \\, P(3), \\, \\ldots,$ where*\n\n- *$P(1)$ is true, and*\n\n- *$P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$*\n\n*Then, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$*\n\nLet's recall our general strategy for proving results using induction:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how induction is used to prove inequality statements. But first, let's discuss an important property of numbers often used in these proofs." :tutorial/id #uuid "c585281c-7bd0-57d6-b354-43229b6e8cf7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7933 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2e905cb3-e114-5070-9e69-88067966fecf" :step/math-academy-id 182504 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093498 :tutorial/content "When proving inequalities using induction, we often use a property of integers known as **transitivity of inequality.**\n\nIn particular, if $a, b,$ and $c$ are integers, then the transitivity of inequality states the following:\n\n- If $a > b$ and $b > c,$ then $a > c$\n\n- If $a \\geq b$ and $b \\geq c,$ then $a \\geq c$\n\n- If $a < b$ and $b < c,$ then $a < c$\n\n- If $a \\leq b$ and $b \\leq c,$ then $a \\leq c$\n\nFor example:\n\n- Since $3 > 2$ and $2 > 1,$ transitivity of inequality implies that $3 > 1.$\n\n- Since $2 \\leq 3$ and $3 \\leq 5,$ then transitivity of inequality implies that $2 \\leq 5.$\n\nLet's now see how induction can be used to prove an inequality statement." :tutorial/id #uuid "9295857c-f317-5f8b-a656-9bcf405921e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9170 :tutorial/title "Transitivity of Inequality"} :step/id #uuid "a6c5f326-acbf-56e7-a8fa-2ee4f6d2ce6b" :step/math-academy-id 182505 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093004}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093497 :tutorial/content "Let's prove that the following statement is true using mathematical induction:\n\n$n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$P(n): \\quad n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n \\in\\mathbb N$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n \\geq 1,$ the base case is $P(1).$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}+3(1)=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)-1=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, since $4 > 1,$ our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nk^2+3k > 2k-1.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+1)^2+ 3(k+1) > 2(k+1)-1 = 2k+1 \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{2}+3(k+1) & ={k}^{2}+2k+1+3k+3 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+3k+2k+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+3k+2k+4 & ={{({k}^{2}+3k)}_{⏟}}_{>2k-1}+2k+4 \\\\  & >2k-1+2k+4 \\\\  & =(2k+2)+(2k+1) \\\\  & ={{2k+2}_{⏟}}_{>0}+2k+1 \\\\  & >2k+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n \\in \\mathbb N.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "34de351e-9875-51d8-954e-c36039a677e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9168 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "e8c2174f-bc0e-5501-bccc-c8d28d3a0a3f" :step/math-academy-id 182506 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093005}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093005 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092189 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091943 :question/id #uuid "493f9465-dfd4-5103-8aba-e02d92282172" :question/math-academy-id "e-12674"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e61d3403-80d9-5633-b579-dcf452dbc265" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048631}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413946}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"} :step/id #uuid "4ef74ebf-152a-5ae1-9441-667c8f0dda9a" :step/math-academy-id 182507 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093006}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092190 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091949 :question/id #uuid "6b8b1014-56bf-51c0-be9e-a194181a0d99" :question/math-academy-id "e-13225"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b83c640f-c7fd-5adc-94b8-b129d357b140" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048677}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092616}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Polynomial Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "7a2f8cf6-aca3-5e93-8f24-9846e22e5d40" :step/math-academy-id 182510 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092191 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091950 :question/id #uuid "f5d8d9e3-d45c-5ad9-aae7-7933e6f4fade" :question/math-academy-id "e-13227"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82830730-a9bc-57bd-9114-d0d50473ea63" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048543}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092615}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Other Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "1a66ff57-4f1e-549c-9e1e-95f385befca5" :step/math-academy-id 182513}] :activity/title "Proving Inequalities Using Induction" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092428}]}
